A1. PROJECT DEFINITION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Name: LifeBridge AI
Tagline: Connecting Lives Through Intelligent Organ Donation.
One-line description: an organ-aware, explainable and human-supervised
transplant coordination platform that combines configurable organ-specific
decision support with logistics monitoring, fairness analysis and verifiable
auditability, without replacing authorized clinical or allocation decisions.

Lifecycle supported: donor registration > organ-specific consent > medical
and document verification > recipient registration per organ > organ
availability > data-quality check > AI-assisted eligibility and ranking >
explainable recommendations > human-reviewed allocation > transport and
preservation monitoring > chain of custody > emergency escalation >
transplant completion > post-transplant recovery > fairness and audit
reporting.

Primary users to optimize first: (1) Transplant Coordinator, the central
operational user; (2) Doctor / transplant surgeon, the clinical decision-
maker; (3) Hospital administrator, who manages hospital operations and staff.
Donors and recipients are public-facing users; platform administrators
oversee the system.

LifeBridge AI does not replace India's official organ-allocation
authorities (NOTTO, ROTTO, SOTTO and hospital protocols). It is a decision-
support and coordination platform. Allocation and clinical decisions remain
under authorized human and regulatory control.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A2. NON-NEGOTIABLE PRINCIPLES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. AI is decision support only. It never allocates an organ, approves a
   transfer, or makes a medical decision. Every allocation requires human
   review and an authorized decision recorded with identity, time, reason
   and model/config version.
2. All patient, donor, hospital and document data is SYNTHETIC and fictional.
   Show a persistent banner: "Demonstration system - synthetic data only.
   Not for real clinical use."
3. Do not invent medically valid compatibility rules, allocation weights,
   preservation limits, survival estimates or expected-benefit numbers. All
   project rules, weights and preservation windows are ILLUSTRATIVE demo
   values stored in versioned configuration, labelled "illustrative demo
   configuration, not an official clinical rule" wherever shown.
4. Never present an AI recommendation as a guaranteed match. Never imply a
   countdown determines clinical viability. Always show: "Clinical viability
   must be assessed by authorized clinicians."
5. Mandatory eligibility is separate from ranking. A candidate who fails an
   eligibility rule never becomes eligible because of a high score.
6. Missing data is never silently scored as zero. It is flagged for
   authorized review and shown as "missing / unverified".
7. No cross-organ ranking. Candidates are only compared within the same
   organ type.
8. No fake buttons, decorative controls or hardcoded success messages. Every
   control runs real logic and shows real results or real errors.
9. No claim of legal or regulatory certification (organ transplant law,
   NOTTO, data protection). Design with them in mind and document gaps.
10. Do not use protected or sensitive characteristics as ranking bonuses or
    penalties. Do not claim a statistical disparity proves discrimination,
    or that equal rates guarantee fairness.
11. Security and privacy rules in PART B apply from the first file onward.

