A7. THE FEATURES AS INTEGRATED MODULES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
F1 EXPLAINABLE MATCHING (organ-wise). Pipeline per organ:
   1) Data-quality gate (see F11). 2) Retrieve candidates from active
   registrations of that organ type only. 3) Apply mandatory eligibility
   ruleset (blood-group compatibility, size/age bands, hospital capability
   and readiness, preservation feasibility, verified consent and documents,
   as configured). 4) Rank ONLY eligible candidates with transparent
   additive factors from the organ config. 5) Output per candidate:
   factor contributions, eligibility checklist, missing/unverified items,
   excluded candidates with reasons, config and ruleset versions, timestamp.
   Show a detailed explanation view for medical reviewers. Keep illustrative
   weights editable only through new config versions.
   This is the most visually distinctive screen in the app.

F2 HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP APPROVAL AS A STATE MACHINE
   States: ORGAN_REGISTERED > MATCHING_COMPLETED > COORDINATOR_REVIEW >
   MEDICAL_REVIEW > AUTHORIZED_DECISION > TRANSPORT_APPROVED >
   TRANSPLANT_COMPLETED, plus REJECTED, WITHDRAWN, CANCELLED, ESCALATED and
   NEEDS_VERIFICATION with explicitly defined allowed transitions. The
   server rejects any undefined transition. Every transition stores actor,
   timestamp, previous state, new state, reason and supporting documents
   where required. Overrides of AI ranking need a mandatory reason
   (minimum length). Separation of duties: whoever creates or runs a
   recommendation cannot give the final medical approval; coordinator and
   medical review must be different users. AI never moves a case forward.

F3 EVENT-DRIVEN LOGISTICS AND PRESERVATION MONITORING
   State is derived from an append-only LogisticsEvents stream: retrieval
   completed, preservation started, transport assigned, vehicle departed,
   route delay detected, custody handover completed, recipient hospital
   readiness confirmed, organ received. A scheduled check evaluates
   configured warning thresholds and raises alerts. Live elegant countdown
   shows organ ID, retrieval time, elapsed time, estimated arrival,
   preservation window (illustrative), transport risk, and current
   custodian. Suggest operational alternatives only to authorized
   coordinators. A countdown never declares an organ viable or non-viable.

F4 COUNTERFACTUAL WHAT-IF SIMULATOR
   Compare hypothetical scenarios (Candidate A vs B) on eligibility, urgency,
   waiting time, transport duration, preservation considerations, fairness
   indicators and operational constraints, using comparison cards, charts,
   scenario timelines and factor-change explanations. Label everything
   "Hypothetical". A simulation can never become an allocation. Omit
   expected benefit or survival entirely (no validated model exists) and
   state why.

F5 FAIRNESS AND BIAS MONITORING
   Aggregate metrics, per organ type: waiting-time distribution,
   recommendation distribution, offer acceptance, regional and hospital
   differences, outcomes, performance across relevant subgroups. Detect
   potential statistical disparities with stated method, sample size and
   limits; compare config versions; flag insufficient data; suppress groups
   below a minimum size; export CSV/PDF reports. State limitations on the
   page.

F6 CONSENT LIFECYCLE AND PRIVACY CONTROL CENTER
   Organ-specific preferences, document submission, status, timestamp,
   version, authorized verification, modification, withdrawal, complete
   history. Consent is never permanent merely because a user registered.
   Withdrawal immediately excludes the donor/organ from new matching runs.

F7 TAMPER-EVIDENT AUDIT TRAIL (see A8).

F8 ORGAN CHAIN OF CUSTODY
   Timeline: donor hospital > authorized retrieval team > transport team >
   recipient hospital > authorized surgical team. At each handover record
   organ ID, previous and new custodian, timestamp, location, preservation/
   temperature data if available, condition assessment, confirming identity
   and notes. Confirm handover, view history, detect missing handovers,
   flag unexpected delays, generate custody reports. Clear vertical timeline.

F9 EMERGENCY ESCALATION AND ALTERNATE-CANDIDATE REVIEW
   Rule-driven triggers: recipient hospital unavailable, confirmation
   timeout, transport delay, missing handover, preservation threshold
   warning, candidate withdrawal or ineligibility. Detect > alert > notify
   authorized coordinators > re-evaluate feasibility > request review of
   alternate eligible candidates > record every action. Configurable levels.
   Never silently replace a recipient or change allocation order; changes
   follow the normal approval path.

F10 POST-TRANSPLANT RECOVERY TRACKER ("digital twin")
   Organ-specific RecoveryPlans: appointments, medication schedules, lab
   report records, recovery indicators, clinician notes, missed
   appointments, patient-reported updates. Timeline, reminders, doctor
   dashboard, trend charts, missed-follow-up alerts, patient education. Any
   AI-generated warning is labelled decision support and reviewed by an
   authorized professional. No diagnoses, no prescribing.

F11 DATA-QUALITY AND MISSING-INFORMATION GATE (before every matching run)
   Check: missing mandatory medical information, expired or unverified
   documents, inconsistent timestamps, invalid organ registration,
   unverified consent, incomplete hospital information. Create
   DataQualityIssues, block or flag affected candidates, and route them to
   authorized review. Never convert missing data to a zero score.

F12 HOSPITAL READINESS AND CAPABILITY MANAGEMENT
   Per hospital and organ type: transplant capability, authorized team
   availability, facilities and readiness status with timestamps. Matching
   and transport recommendations only consider hospitals that are capable
   and ready; stale readiness data is flagged.

