- The browser calls OUR endpoint, which calls Gemini: authenticated, rate
  limited, input-size limited, fixed prompt template, so it cannot be abused
  as a free general proxy.
- .env.example with placeholders only. .gitignore covers .env, .env.*, *.pem,
  *.key, service-account JSON, uploads/, local database files, anchors with
  secrets.

B2. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
- Validate all required variables at startup with Zod; fail fast with a clear
  message that never prints values.
- Separate development and production config. Production refuses to start if
  secrets are missing, default, short or placeholder (JWT secret, HMAC audit
  key, encryption key).
- Document every variable (name, purpose, format, required/optional).

B3. AUTHENTICATION
- Argon2id (or bcrypt cost 12+) with unique salts. Never store plain text.
- Password policy (min 12 chars, common-password check), lockout or
  exponential delay after repeated failures, generic "Invalid credentials".
- Short-lived access tokens (about 15 min) and rotating refresh tokens in
  httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies, not localStorage. Logout invalidates
  the session server-side. Idle and absolute expiry. Re-authentication for
  sensitive actions (role change, consent withdrawal, approvals, config
  publishing).
- Optional TOTP MFA for Admin, Coordinator and Medical roles.
- Single-use, expiring password-reset tokens.
- Public registration creates only Donor or Recipient accounts. Staff,
  coordinator, doctor and admin accounts are created or approved by an
  Admin. Registration can never choose a privileged role.

B4. AUTHORIZATION, ADMIN PROTECTION, OWN-DATA ACCESS
- Deny by default. Every endpoint checks authentication, then permission,
  then ownership or scope (own record, own hospital, assigned case).
  Defend against IDOR: never trust an ID alone; verify the caller may access
  that exact object.
- Never trust client-supplied role, userId, hospitalId, isAdmin or status
  fields; derive identity from the verified session. Use allow-lists for
  updatable fields to prevent mass assignment.
- Admin routes behind a separate guard, MFA where enabled, rate-limited,
  logged and audited. The last Admin cannot be removed or demoted. Role
  changes invalidate existing sessions.
- Separation of duties enforced server-side (recommendation creator cannot
  give final medical approval; coordinator and medical reviewer differ).
- Automated NEGATIVE tests: a Donor cannot read another donor; a Recipient
  cannot read clinical notes or other recipients; hospital staff cannot see
  another hospital's cases; non-admins get 403 on admin routes; a
  coordinator cannot perform a medical approval; tampered IDs, roles and
  invalid state transitions are rejected; unauthenticated calls get 401.
- Fallback with Firestore: never "allow read, write: if true"; per-collection
  rules, role claims, field validation; no client writes to audit, approvals
  or role fields; test with the emulator.

B5. FORM SANITIZATION, VALIDATION, XSS, CSRF, UPLOADS
- Validate every input on client and server with Zod (type, length, format,
  enum, range); reject unknown fields; limit body size.
- Never render user input as raw HTML. No dangerouslySetInnerHTML,
  innerHTML, eval, new Function or document.write. If rich text is needed,
  sanitize with DOMPurify using a strict allow-list.
- Allow-list URLs (http/https only; no javascript: or data:). Prevent open
  redirects.
- File uploads: allow-list extensions and MIME types, verify magic bytes,
  size limits, random server-generated filenames, store outside the web
  root, never execute, serve through an authorized endpoint with
  Content-Disposition and X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, quarantine/scan
  in production, strip image metadata.
- ORM/parameterized queries only; no string-built queries; validate types to
  block NoSQL operator injection.
- CSRF protection for cookie-based sessions on all state-changing requests.

B6. RATE LIMITING AND ABUSE CONTROL
- Global limiter plus strict limiters on login, registration, password
  reset, MFA, uploads, matching runs, report export and the Gemini proxy.
  Return 429 with Retry-After. Key by IP and by user when authenticated.
- Honeypot or CAPTCHA hook on public forms. Cap pagination size and query
  complexity.

B7. CORS, SECURITY HEADERS, DEBUG MODE
- CORS allow-list of origins from environment variables; never "*" with
  credentials; restrict methods and headers.
- helmet or equivalent: Content-Security-Policy (no unsafe-inline or
  unsafe-eval; nonces/hashes; restrict script, style, img, connect,
  frame-ancestors), Strict-Transport-Security, X-Content-Type-Options:
  nosniff, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin,
  Permissions-Policy (disable camera, microphone, geolocation unless
  needed), Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy, frame-ancestors 'none' or 'self'.
  Remove X-Powered-By.
- Debug off in production: no stack traces, verbose errors, SQL errors,
  source maps, dev tools, test routes, API playgrounds, or seed/admin
  utilities. Use a generic error handler that returns an error ID and logs
  details server-side with redaction.
- Cookies Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax/Strict. Force HTTPS in production.

B8. DEPENDENCIES AND UNUSED PACKAGES
- Run npm audit and report the results. Update vulnerable packages, keep a
  lockfile, prefer maintained libraries, verify package names (typosquats).
- Remove unused packages (depcheck/knip), dead code, unused routes, demo
  endpoints and scaffolding; report what was removed. Add audit and depcheck
  scripts.

B9. EXPOSED FILES AND BUILD OUTPUT
- Public web root contains only intended assets. Block access to .env, .git,
  config, package files, logs, database files, backups, uploads, source maps
  and dotfiles. No directory listing.
- Disable production source maps. Verify the bundle has no secrets, demo
  accounts, internal URLs or debug data.
- robots.txt and sitemap reveal no sensitive paths. Add security.txt.

B10. DATABASE SECURITY
- Least-privilege database user; no default or shared credentials; database
  not publicly reachable; TLS where supported.
- Field-level encryption at rest for sensitive fields (contact details,
  identifiers, medical notes) with a key from the environment/secret
  manager; never log them.
- Tracked migrations; synthetic, idempotent seeds; encrypted backups with a
  documented, tested restore procedure; soft deletion and retention periods;
  user data export and deletion flow.
- Unique, foreign-key, NOT NULL and CHECK constraints. Audit tables
  protected from UPDATE/DELETE by privileges or triggers.

B11. SECRETS IN GIT HISTORY
- Before any commit or push: verify .gitignore, run a secret scanner
  (gitleaks or trufflehog) on the working tree AND full history, and add a
  pre-commit hook that blocks secrets.
- If any secret was ever committed, pasted into a prompt, chat or log, treat
  it as compromised: rotate it FIRST, then remove it from history
  (git filter-repo or BFG) and force-push. Deleting the file in a new commit
  is not enough. Document the runbook in docs/SECURITY.md.

B12. PRIVACY AND DATA PROTECTION
- Data minimization and purpose limitation per field; plain-language
  privacy notice; versioned, timestamped consent text.
- Role-based field-level masking (hospital staff see partial identifiers;
  coordinators see what the workflow needs; only assigned clinicians see
  clinical detail).
- No sensitive data in URLs, query strings, localStorage, analytics, error
  messages, notifications, emails, audit payloads or logs.
- Fairness reports use aggregates with minimum group-size thresholds.
- User-rights flows: view own data, correct, export, request deletion or
  withdrawal (subject to legal retention), and grievance contact.
- Design with India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, the
  Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act 1994, NOTTO and
  institutional protocols in mind; document the gaps; claim no compliance
  or certification.

B13. LOGGING AND MONITORING
- Structured logs with request IDs, no secrets or PII. Security events
  (failed logins, permission denials, rate-limit hits, role changes,
  integrity failures) stored in SecurityEvents and surfaced to Admin.
- Audit verification on demand and on a schedule with Admin alert on failure.
- Timeouts and size limits on all outbound calls.

B14. SECURITY TESTS TO WRITE AND RUN
Authorization/IDOR per role; mass assignment (cannot set role, isAdmin,
hospitalId, status); separation of duties; XSS payloads in every text field
render inert; SQL/NoSQL injection payloads rejected; rate limits return 429;
disallowed CORS origin gets no CORS headers; security headers present on
HTML and API responses; upload tests (wrong type, oversized, double
extension, polyglot); session tests (expiry, logout invalidation, token
reuse after rotation); audit-chain tamper test; secret scan of repo and
bundle; production-mode test (no stack traces, source maps or debug
routes). Write docs/SECURITY.md with a threat-model summary, controls
table, test results and remaining risks, stated honestly.


